  "result": "Perfect! The payment terms implementation is complete and all tests pass. Here's a summary of what was added:\n\n## Changes made:\n\n**1. Updated `src/errors.js`**\n- Added `TERMS_INVALID`: \"Terms must be NET14, NET30, or EOM.\"\n\n**2. Updated `src/invoices.js`**\n- Imported `addDays` from `dates.js`\n- Added `terms` parameter to `createInvoice` with default value `'NET30'`\n- Added validation to ensure terms is one of: `'NET14'`, `'NET30'`, or `'EOM'`\n- Calculate `dueDate` based on terms:\n  - **NET14**: 14 days after issue date (using `addDays`)\n  - **NET30**: 30 days after issue date (using `addDays`)\n  - **EOM**: Last day of the month of the issue date (calculated using JavaScript Date UTC)\n- Returns both `terms` and `dueDate` in the invoice object\n\n**3. Added 18 new tests to `test/invoices.test.js`** covering:\n- Default NET30 terms and correct due date calculation\n- All three term types (NET14, NET30, EOM)\n- Edge cases for EOM:\n  - First and last days of month\n  - February in leap year (Feb 29) and non-leap year (Feb 28)\n  - 30-day months (April, June, etc.)\n  - 31-day months (December, etc.)\n- Month/year boundary crossing for NET14 and NET30\n- Error handling (TERMS_INVALID for unknown, empty, or case-sensitive terms)\n- Validation order (terms validated before lines)\n\n**Test results**: All 171 tests pass (153 existing + 18 new)",
